1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,072
It's Tuesday morning, and your CTO asks:
is it the model, the data, or our own

2
00:00:09,112 --> 00:00:13,979
harness? Which commit, who wrote it, why,
and is it in the release that went out on

3
00:00:14,019 --> 00:00:14,393
Friday?

4
00:00:14,733 --> 00:00:19,128
You open a terminal and type git
log. Thirty-odd commits scroll past.

5
00:00:19,168 --> 00:00:24,462
A commit is one saved snapshot of the whole
project, with a note about who saved it

6
00:00:24,502 --> 00:00:29,154
and why. You've read all of them and
answered none of the four questions.

7
00:00:29,194 --> 00:00:34,552
Then you try to narrow it down by date, you
type --since=2026-09-10, and the commits

8
00:00:34,592 --> 00:00:37,830
from the morning of the
tenth aren't in the output.

9
00:00:37,870 --> 00:00:43,164
Nothing is wrong with the repository. The
query was wrong, and Git didn't tell you.

10
00:00:43,504 --> 00:00:48,188
This video is about asking git log a
question it can answer, and knowing why the

11
00:00:48,228 --> 00:00:51,495
answer is what it is. Keep
that missing morning in mind.

12
00:00:51,535 --> 00:00:52,321
It comes back.

13
00:00:54,161 --> 00:00:57,887
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:57,927 --> 00:01:02,764
In the previous video you read diffs. A
diff, the lines removed and added, is a

15
00:01:02,804 --> 00:01:06,714
query over a pair of snapshots.
Today's query is over the graph.

16
00:01:06,754 --> 00:01:11,838
Every commit names the commit it was built
on, its parent, and those links form the

17
00:01:11,878 --> 00:01:16,344
graph. Which commits are in this set,
and what do I print about each one?

18
00:01:16,684 --> 00:01:21,874
You'll work in scorekit, a small library
that scores model answers against reference

19
00:01:21,914 --> 00:01:26,980
answers. Its history was prepared for
Chapter 14A: nine days, three authors, three

20
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:32,023
renames, one formatter commit, one deleted
file, a merge that joined two lines of

21
00:01:32,063 --> 00:01:35,883
work, a squash merge, an unmerged
branch, and two regressions.

22
00:01:35,923 --> 00:01:41,051
You'll stay in this repository for the next
five videos, so it's worth learning its

23
00:01:41,091 --> 00:01:41,673
shape now.

24
00:01:42,013 --> 00:01:44,990
Everything on screen comes
from four replay scripts.

25
00:01:45,030 --> 00:01:49,632
The lab clock is fixed, so the commit IDs
here are the ones in the book, and the

26
00:01:49,672 --> 00:01:52,185
ones you'll get when
you replay the scripts.

27
00:01:54,025 --> 00:01:55,603
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:01:55,943 --> 00:02:01,089
describe a git log invocation as start
points, exclusions, filters, format;

29
00:02:01,429 --> 00:02:06,195
filter history by author, message, date
and path, and say which date is compared;

30
00:02:06,535 --> 00:02:12,482
show integrations only with --first-parent,
and merges only with --merges;

31
00:02:12,822 --> 00:02:15,830
produce a custom one-line format
and a per-author summary;

32
00:02:16,170 --> 00:02:19,583
explain why a date filter can
omit commits from the named day.

33
00:02:21,423 --> 00:02:26,138
Start with why. History stores snapshots
and parent links, nothing else.

34
00:02:26,178 --> 00:02:29,571
On screen: seven of today's
commits and their links.

35
00:02:29,611 --> 00:02:34,920
Diffs, renames, "who wrote this line" and
"which commit broke it" aren't recorded

36
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:40,071
anywhere. Git computes them when you ask.
So an investigation is a sequence of

37
00:02:40,111 --> 00:02:45,617
queries, and git log is the query engine
for the first kind: queries over the graph.

38
00:02:45,957 --> 00:02:53,012
In one sentence: 🟢 SAFE, git log walks the
graph from the tips you name, filters the

39
00:02:53,052 --> 00:02:58,756
commits it meets, and prints each
survivor in the format you choose.

40
00:02:58,796 --> 00:03:05,091
A tip is the commit that a branch, a
name that moves forward as you commit,

41
00:03:05,131 --> 00:03:08,976
currently holds. It reads.
It changes nothing.

42
00:03:09,316 --> 00:03:13,468
Precisely, think in four stages, and
place every option in one of them.

43
00:03:13,808 --> 00:03:20,985
Stage one is the walk: which commits are
visited. Revisions and ranges belong here,

44
00:03:21,025 --> 00:03:27,419
and so do --all, --branches, --reflog,
--first-parent and --ancestry-path.

45
00:03:27,759 --> 00:03:33,594
Stage two is the filter: which of
the visited commits are shown.

46
00:03:33,634 --> 00:03:41,214
--author, --committer, --grep, --since,
--until, --merges, --no-merges, the pickaxe

47
00:03:41,254 --> 00:03:48,467
options -S and -G, a path after two dashes,
--diff-filter, and the count limit.

48
00:03:48,807 --> 00:03:55,919
Stage three is the order: --date-order,
--topo-order, --author-date-order,

49
00:03:55,959 --> 00:03:56,886
--reverse.

50
00:03:57,226 --> 00:04:06,437
Stage four is the print: --oneline,
--format, --graph, --decorate, --stat, -p,

51
00:04:06,477 --> 00:04:08,097
--name-status.

52
00:04:08,437 --> 00:04:14,782
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: take
the command you already use most, git log

53
00:04:14,822 --> 00:04:20,407
--graph --oneline --decorate --all.
Which stages do its options come from?

54
00:04:20,447 --> 00:04:22,156
Write your answer down.

55
00:04:26,996 --> 00:04:30,490
It's one option from each of
stages one, three and four.

56
00:04:30,530 --> 00:04:35,664
Walk from every ref. A ref is a name that
holds a commit ID, such as a branch or a

57
00:04:35,704 --> 00:04:40,902
tag. Order topologically and draw the edges.
Print one line per commit with the ref

58
00:04:40,942 --> 00:04:41,281
names.

59
00:04:41,621 --> 00:04:47,113
When to think this way: always, and say
it out loud when you explain a query to a

60
00:04:47,153 --> 00:04:52,850
colleague. "git log --author=asha -5" is
"walk from HEAD, keep Asha's commits, print

61
00:04:52,890 --> 00:04:57,494
the first five that pass". HEAD is
Git's name for where you are now.

62
00:04:57,534 --> 00:05:01,250
The limit counts after the
filter and before --reverse.

63
00:05:01,290 --> 00:05:05,894
That's why --reverse -1 prints the
newest commit and not the oldest.

64
00:05:05,934 --> 00:05:11,563
If you want the root commits, the ones
without a parent, the textbook gives you git

65
00:05:11,603 --> 00:05:13,612
rev-list --max-parents=0 HEAD.

66
00:05:13,952 --> 00:05:17,278
When the model fails you, it
fails in one of three places.

67
00:05:17,318 --> 00:05:20,993
You walked from the wrong tips,
so the commit was never visited.

68
00:05:21,033 --> 00:05:24,534
Your filter tested something
other than what you had in mind.

69
00:05:24,574 --> 00:05:28,306
Or the output hid a distinction,
such as author versus committer.

70
00:05:28,346 --> 00:05:31,325
You'll see one example of
each in the demonstration.

71
00:05:33,165 --> 00:05:37,773
The textbook's analogy is a database
query. FROM is the set of commits.

72
00:05:37,813 --> 00:05:42,684
WHERE is the filter. ORDER BY is the
ordering. SELECT is the output format.

73
00:05:43,024 --> 00:05:46,515
The analogy breaks in two places,
and both matter in production.

74
00:05:46,855 --> 00:05:53,517
First, there's no index on authors or
messages. Every filter is a scan of the walked

75
00:05:53,557 --> 00:05:59,342
commits. A filter never makes the walk
smaller. Only stage one does that.

76
00:05:59,682 --> 00:06:03,279
Second, the default order is
by commit date, newest first.

77
00:06:03,319 --> 00:06:08,171
Only --topo-order, which --graph implies,
guarantees that no parent is printed

78
00:06:08,211 --> 00:06:13,126
before all of its children. If you read
a plain git log as "this happened, then

79
00:06:13,166 --> 00:06:16,450
this", you're reading commit
dates and not the graph.

80
00:06:18,790 --> 00:06:22,655
Keep the four words on screen in
mind: walk, filter, order, print.

81
00:06:22,695 --> 00:06:27,447
For every command in the rest of this
video, name the stage of each option before

82
00:06:27,487 --> 00:06:28,808
you look at the output.

83
00:06:30,648 --> 00:06:36,540
First pass: shade all seven commits. The
range 8e2cac1~1..8657273 walks from the

84
00:06:36,580 --> 00:06:40,395
merge back to, and excluding,
the parent of 8e2cac1.

85
00:06:40,435 --> 00:06:46,328
A merge commit has more than one parent.
Both parents of the merge are followed.

86
00:06:46,668 --> 00:06:52,204
Second pass: only the bottom row.
--first-parent is a stage-one option.

87
00:06:52,244 --> 00:06:58,174
At the merge 8657273 the walk takes the
first parent, 389337a, the side that

88
00:06:58,214 --> 00:07:03,830
received the merge, and never visits
the two commits of the side branch.

89
00:07:03,870 --> 00:07:06,108
On screen, those two go dark.

90
00:07:06,448 --> 00:07:11,191
Third pass: the merge alone.
--merges is a stage-two option.

91
00:07:11,231 --> 00:07:16,055
All seven commits were walked,
so all seven are shaded again.

92
00:07:16,095 --> 00:07:22,433
Six were filtered out because they have
one parent, and only 8657273 is printed.

93
00:07:22,773 --> 00:07:27,653
Same range, three questions, three answers.
Two of the options changed the walk or

94
00:07:27,693 --> 00:07:30,054
the filter. None changed the repository.

95
00:07:31,894 --> 00:07:33,115
Start with the symptom.

96
00:07:33,455 --> 00:07:39,237
Predict: what will the exit status of the
runner be, given a pass mark and a score

97
00:07:39,277 --> 00:07:39,876
of 0.400?

98
00:07:40,216 --> 00:07:44,790
Point at the exit status 1:
exact_match is below the pass mark.

99
00:07:44,830 --> 00:07:50,503
Then at git describe: the current commit
is five commits after the tag v0.2.0.

100
00:07:50,543 --> 00:07:54,677
A tag is a name for a commit
that isn't expected to move.

101
00:07:55,017 --> 00:07:59,281
Now the whole history, with one option
from each of stages one, three and four.

102
00:07:59,621 --> 00:08:04,254
Read it from the bottom. Two
tags, v0.1.0 and v0.2.0.

103
00:08:04,294 --> 00:08:11,131
One true merge, 8657273. Two branches
that were never merged, feat/rouge-l and

104
00:08:11,171 --> 00:08:18,273
feat/report. Somewhere between v0.1.0,
which the team knows scored correctly, and

105
00:08:18,313 --> 00:08:23,651
main there are twenty commits.
You'll count them in a moment.

106
00:08:26,991 --> 00:08:34,889
That's the diagram you saw, as Git draws
it. Predict before the next command: with

107
00:08:34,929 --> 00:08:40,601
--first-parent, how many lines?
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

108
00:08:45,441 --> 00:08:50,423
Five. On a branch that receives work through
merges, this is the sequence of things

109
00:08:50,463 --> 00:08:54,174
that landed. The commits inside
each landed branch are hidden.

110
00:08:54,514 --> 00:09:00,794
Twenty commits since v0.1.0. Eighteen on
the first-parent line, because two arrived

111
00:09:00,834 --> 00:09:04,830
through the merge. Nineteen
without the merge itself.

112
00:09:04,870 --> 00:09:10,693
Keep the number twenty. In the bisect
videos it becomes the cost of a search.

113
00:09:11,033 --> 00:09:15,227
For a repository you don't know, one more
stage-two option gives you the outline.

114
00:09:15,567 --> 00:09:18,700
Only the commits that a branch
or tag points at remain.

115
00:09:19,040 --> 00:09:23,837
--author and --committer are regular
expressions, patterns for matching text,

116
00:09:23,877 --> 00:09:28,736
matched against "Name, email in angle
brackets". The second command asks for a

117
00:09:28,776 --> 00:09:33,635
commit written by one person and committed
by another, and finds one: e376e5b.

118
00:09:33,675 --> 00:09:38,723
That's the cherry-pick. A cherry-pick
copies one commit's change onto the current

119
00:09:38,763 --> 00:09:43,308
branch as a new commit. Remember it.
It comes back when we look at dates.

120
00:09:43,648 --> 00:09:44,410
By message.

121
00:09:44,750 --> 00:09:49,790
Predict two things. Does the second
command find the commits on feat/rouge-l?

122
00:09:49,830 --> 00:09:54,607
And a quick quiz, two options: do two
--grep options mean "and", or "or"?

123
00:09:54,647 --> 00:09:55,663
Say it out loud.

124
00:10:00,503 --> 00:10:05,854
The quiz first: "or". Several --grep
options are alternatives unless you add

125
00:10:05,894 --> 00:10:11,741
--all-match. And --grep searches messages,
not content: the BLEU removal d9d075d is

126
00:10:11,781 --> 00:10:15,217
listed only because its
message mentions ROUGE-L.

127
00:10:15,557 --> 00:10:20,902
And the first prediction: no. Without --all
the walk starts at HEAD, so commits that

128
00:10:20,942 --> 00:10:26,222
exist only on other branches aren't searched.
That's a stage-one mistake that looks

129
00:10:26,262 --> 00:10:31,286
like a stage-two result. With --all, two
commits on feat/rouge-l match as well.

130
00:10:31,626 --> 00:10:32,231
By date.

131
00:10:32,571 --> 00:10:36,412
Five commits on the tenth, with
full timestamps in the query.

132
00:10:36,452 --> 00:10:41,565
Dates have two traps. The first: --since
and --until test the committer date, the

133
00:10:41,605 --> 00:10:45,128
time the commit object was
created, not the author date.

134
00:10:45,468 --> 00:10:48,927
e376e5b was authored at 10:46
and committed at 12:41.

135
00:10:48,967 --> 00:10:53,879
It passes a filter of "since 12:30"
because the committer date is compared.

136
00:10:53,919 --> 00:10:59,358
A cherry-picked commit, or a rebased one,
which is also a new copy, carries its old

137
00:10:59,398 --> 00:11:01,801
author date and a new committer date.

138
00:11:02,141 --> 00:11:06,328
Now stop. This is the snippet
the video is built around.

139
00:11:06,368 --> 00:11:08,291
The lab clock reads 12:51.

140
00:11:08,631 --> 00:11:12,987
You want the commits of the tenth of
September, and you write the dates without a

141
00:11:13,027 --> 00:11:16,568
time. You saw five commits a minute
ago. Predict what this prints.

142
00:11:21,408 --> 00:11:26,038
Two commits, and one of them, 9c8df98,
was made on the morning of the eleventh.

143
00:11:26,078 --> 00:11:29,289
Four commits from the morning
of the tenth are missing.

144
00:11:29,329 --> 00:11:30,944
There's the missing morning.

145
00:11:31,284 --> 00:11:37,217
Here it is as a picture. With no time given,
the window runs from 12:51 on the tenth

146
00:11:37,257 --> 00:11:40,132
to 12:51 on the eleventh. Watch it slide.

147
00:11:40,472 --> 00:11:46,138
And the caveat, exactly as the textbook
states it: this behavior was observed on Git

148
00:11:46,178 --> 00:11:51,572
2.55.0, in the transcript you have on
screen. The manual page of git log doesn't

149
00:11:51,612 --> 00:11:55,648
describe it. Almost everyone
gets this wrong the first time.

150
00:11:55,988 --> 00:12:01,156
There's a third property of --since. It
stops the walk at the first commit that is

151
00:12:01,196 --> 00:12:06,110
too old. In a history whose commit dates
aren't in order, for example after an

152
00:12:06,150 --> 00:12:11,318
import or with a wrong clock, an old-looking
commit can hide newer ones behind it.

153
00:12:11,358 --> 00:12:14,430
--since-as-filter visits
the whole range instead.

154
00:12:14,770 --> 00:12:15,455
By path.

155
00:12:15,795 --> 00:12:20,777
git log -- <path> shows the commits whose
version of the path differs from their

156
00:12:20,817 --> 00:12:25,799
parent's. With merges in the history, the
default mode also prunes side branches

157
00:12:25,839 --> 00:12:30,821
that didn't contribute to the final content,
so a change that was made and later

158
00:12:30,861 --> 00:12:35,341
undone on a branch may not appear.
--full-history disables that pruning.

159
00:12:35,381 --> 00:12:40,237
Adding --stat or -p prints the summary
or the patch, and with a path only that

160
00:12:40,277 --> 00:12:40,991
path's part.

161
00:12:49,331 --> 00:12:54,203
Abbreviated ID, short author date, the
author padded and truncated to ten columns,

162
00:12:54,243 --> 00:12:59,235
the subject. This one-line format answers
most "who and when" questions at a glance.

163
00:12:59,275 --> 00:13:04,267
In the second command look at the relative
dates: e376e5b says two hours ago and the

164
00:13:04,307 --> 00:13:08,939
commit below it says 32 minutes ago.
That's the author date of the cherry-pick

165
00:13:08,979 --> 00:13:13,971
again. Relative dates are computed from the
clock at the moment you run the command,

166
00:13:14,011 --> 00:13:16,247
so paste absolute dates into a report.

167
00:13:16,587 --> 00:13:19,719
Trailers carry attribution
that the author field can't.

168
00:13:19,759 --> 00:13:24,103
A trailer is a key and value line in
the last paragraph of a commit message.

169
00:13:24,443 --> 00:13:31,085
7823232 is a squash merge: several
commits combined into one new commit.

170
00:13:31,125 --> 00:13:35,262
One author field, two
people in the trailers.

171
00:13:35,602 --> 00:13:41,008
-s counts, -n sorts by count. The second
form adds the Co-authored-by trailer as a

172
00:13:41,048 --> 00:13:46,519
second grouping key, so Asha and Ravi are
credited for the squashed commit as well.

173
00:13:46,559 --> 00:13:50,637
Without -s, shortlog over a range
is a draft of release notes.

174
00:13:50,677 --> 00:13:52,961
You'll produce one in the exercise.

175
00:13:53,301 --> 00:13:58,115
One last command, and a prediction.
Standard input isn't a terminal here.

176
00:13:58,155 --> 00:14:01,440
What does git shortlog -sn
print with no revision?

177
00:14:01,780 --> 00:14:07,041
Nothing. In that situation shortlog reads
a log from standard input instead of

178
00:14:07,081 --> 00:14:12,343
walking from HEAD. In a script, a hook
or a CI job, where no person is typing,

179
00:14:12,383 --> 00:14:14,042
always pass the revision.

180
00:14:15,882 --> 00:14:17,530
Five mistakes to watch for.

181
00:14:17,870 --> 00:14:24,291
A date filter that drops the morning. Root
cause: a date without a time takes the

182
00:14:24,331 --> 00:14:30,910
present time of day from the clock, so
--since=2026-09-10 meant the tenth at 12:51.

183
00:14:31,250 --> 00:14:35,105
"It was committed on Tuesday, why
does the filter not find it?"

184
00:14:35,145 --> 00:14:40,050
Root cause: --since and --until compare
the committer date, and a cherry-pick or

185
00:14:40,090 --> 00:14:44,563
rebase gives a commit a new committer
date while keeping the author date.

186
00:14:44,903 --> 00:14:50,032
A search that misses commits on other
branches. Root cause: without --all the walk

187
00:14:50,072 --> 00:14:53,121
starts at HEAD; the filter
never saw the commits.

188
00:14:53,461 --> 00:14:58,744
git log -- path omits a change you know
was made. Root cause: with merges in the

189
00:14:58,784 --> 00:15:04,133
history the default path mode prunes side
branches that did not contribute to the

190
00:15:04,173 --> 00:15:07,527
final content; --full-history
disables the pruning.

191
00:15:07,867 --> 00:15:12,457
A CI job whose git shortlog prints nothing.
Root cause: with no revision and a

192
00:15:12,497 --> 00:15:17,206
standard input that is not a terminal,
shortlog reads a log from standard input.

193
00:15:17,546 --> 00:15:21,394
A sixth, for judgement and not for
Git: commit counts measure commits.

194
00:15:21,434 --> 00:15:25,616
They say nothing about the size or the
value of the work, and a squash-merge

195
00:15:25,656 --> 00:15:29,060
workflow moves the counts to
whoever presses the merge button.

196
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:35,874
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation team
writes an incident note: "No commits touched

197
00:15:35,914 --> 00:15:40,889
the harness on 10 September." The engineer
ran the date query at lunchtime without a

198
00:15:40,929 --> 00:15:45,844
time of day. Four commits from that morning
weren't listed, and one of them changed

199
00:15:45,884 --> 00:15:47,157
how warnings are sent.

200
00:15:47,497 --> 00:15:52,096
The corrected note states the query as it
was run: the range, a full timestamp at

201
00:15:52,136 --> 00:15:56,679
each end, and the time zone. It also says
which date was compared: the committer

202
00:15:56,719 --> 00:16:01,032
date. Anyone who reads the note can
rerun the command and get the same list,

203
00:16:01,072 --> 00:16:05,499
whatever the time of day. That's the
standard for evidence in the rest of this

204
00:16:05,539 --> 00:16:09,681
course: the command, written so that
it's reproducible, beside the claim.

